<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter9_pointers-and-c-style-arrays">
    <title>C-Style Arrays and Pointers</title>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter9_c-style-arrays_introduction">
        <title>What Is a C-Style Array?</title>

        <p>
            A <term>C-style array</term> is a built-in C++ collection that stores
            a fixed number of elements of one type in consecutive memory
            locations. The elements are numbered from zero, so the first element
            has index <c>0</c> and the last element has index one less than the
            array's length.
        </p>

        <p>
            The following declarations create arrays with five and three
            elements. The empty braces value-initialize every element, so the
            integers begin as zero. When an initializer lists the elements, the
            compiler determines the length when the brackets are empty.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_ch9_c_style_array_declaration">
            <title>Declaring, initializing, and indexing C-style arrays</title>
            <program xml:id="program_ch9_c_style_array_declaration"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int scores[5]{};
    int temperatures[]{68, 71, 75};

    scores[0] = 88;
    scores[1] = 91;

    std::cout << scores[0] << '\n';
    std::cout << scores[1] << '\n';
    std::cout << temperatures[2] << '\n';

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            C-style arrays have a fixed length after they are created. They do
            not provide member functions such as <c>size()</c> or <c>at()</c>,
            and indexing does not automatically check whether an index is in
            range. The programmer must preserve the array's bounds.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_ch9_c_style_array_and_std_array">
            <title>C-style arrays and <c>std::array</c></title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="28%"/>
                <col width="36%"/>
                <col width="36%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Feature</cell>
                    <cell>C-style array</cell>
                    <cell><c>std::array</c></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Length</cell>
                    <cell>Fixed at creation.</cell>
                    <cell>Fixed at creation.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Element access</cell>
                    <cell>Indexing with no automatic bounds checking.</cell>
                    <cell>Indexing, plus <c>at()</c> for checked access.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Length query</cell>
                    <cell>Often calculated with <c>sizeof</c>.</cell>
                    <cell><c>size()</c> reports the number of elements.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Interface</cell>
                    <cell>Built-in language feature.</cell>
                    <cell>Library type with a clearer object interface.</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            In new code, <c>std::array</c> is often easier to use safely. C-style
            arrays remain important because they are part of C++'s history, are
            used by older interfaces, and help explain how pointers and memory
            work.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter9_c-style-arrays_sizeof">
        <title>Counting C-Style Array Elements with <c>sizeof</c></title>

        <p>
            The <c>sizeof</c> operator reports a size in bytes. Applied to an
            actual C-style array, it reports the size of the entire array. Applied
            to one element, it reports the size of that element. Dividing the
            first size by the second gives the number of elements:
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_ch9_c_style_array_sizeof">
            <title>Counting elements with <c>sizeof</c></title>
            <program xml:id="program_ch9_c_style_array_sizeof"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int scores[]{88, 91, 95, 97};

    std::size_t array_bytes = sizeof(scores);
    std::size_t element_bytes = sizeof(scores[0]);
    std::size_t element_count = array_bytes / element_bytes;

    std::cout << "Array bytes: " << array_bytes << '\n';
    std::cout << "Element bytes: " << element_bytes << '\n';
    std::cout << "Element count: " << element_count << '\n';

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            This calculation works only while <c>scores</c> is still an actual
            array in the current scope. When an array is passed to a function,
            it normally converts to a pointer to its first element. Inside that
            function, <c>sizeof(values)</c> measures the pointer, not the whole
            original array. That is why a pointer-based function interface must
            receive the element count separately.
        </p>

        <p>
            The type <c>std::size_t</c> is an unsigned integer type intended for
            object sizes and array indexes. The expression
            <c>sizeof(scores) / sizeof(scores[0])</c> is a traditional way to
            count elements in a C-style array. For new code, <c>std::size(scores)</c>
            or a <c>std::array</c>'s <c>size()</c> member can make the intent
            clearer when those facilities are available.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter9_c-style-arrays_array-to-pointer-conversion">
        <title>Array-to-Pointer Conversion</title>

        <p>
            In many expressions, a C-style array name is converted to a pointer
            to its first element. This conversion is commonly called
            <term>array decay</term>.
        </p>

    <interactive xml:id="interactive_ch9_array_decay_parameter"
                 iframe="https://pythontutor.com/iframe-embed.html#code=%23include%20%3Ciostream%3E%0A%23include%20%3Ccstddef%3E%0A%0Avoid%20print_values%28const%20int*%20values%2C%20std%3A%3Asize_t%20count%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20for%20%28std%3A%3Asize_t%20index%20%3D%200%3B%20index%20%3C%20count%3B%20%2B%2Bindex%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20values%5Bindex%5D%20%3C%3C%20%27%20%27%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%7D%0A%0Aint%20main%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20int%20scores%5B%5D%7B88%2C%2091%2C%2095%7D%3B%0A%0A%20%20%20%20print_values%28scores%2C%203%29%3B%0A%0A%20%20%20%20return%200%3B%0A%7D&amp;codeDivHeight=700&amp;codeDivWidth=350&amp;cppShowMemAddrs=true&amp;curInstr=1&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js&amp;py=cpp_g%2B%2B9.3.0"
                 width="100%"
                 hight="200%">
    </interactive>

    <p>
        When <c>scores</c> is passed to <c>print_values()</c>, it becomes a
        pointer to the first element. The pointer does not carry the array's
        length, so the function receives <c>count</c> separately.
    </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter9_pointers-and-c-style-arrays_pointer-arithmetic">
        <title>Pointer Arithmetic</title>

        <p>
            Pointer arithmetic moves by elements of the pointed-to type, not by
            individual bytes. If an <c>int*</c> points to one array element,
            adding 1 produces a pointer to the next <c>int</c> in that array.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_ch9_pointer_arithmetic_traversal">
            <title>Traversing an array with pointer arithmetic</title>
            <program xml:id="program_ch9_pointer_arithmetic_traversal"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int scores[]{88, 91, 95};
    int* current = scores;

    std::cout << *current << '\n';

    ++current;
    std::cout << *current << '\n';

    std::cout << *(current + 1) << '\n';

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <table xml:id="table_ch9_pointer_arithmetic">
            <title>Common pointer-arithmetic expressions</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="28%"/>
                <col width="34%"/>
                <col width="38%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Expression</cell>
                    <cell>Meaning</cell>
                    <cell>Important condition</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>pointer + 1</c></cell>
                    <cell>Pointer to the next element.</cell>
                    <cell>Must remain within the same array or one position past it.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>++pointer</c></cell>
                    <cell>Move to the next element.</cell>
                    <cell>Do not dereference a one-past-the-end pointer.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>*pointer</c></cell>
                    <cell>Access the pointed-to element.</cell>
                    <cell>The pointer must identify a valid live element.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>pointer[index]</c></cell>
                    <cell>Equivalent to <c>*(pointer + index)</c>.</cell>
                    <cell>The resulting position must be a valid element.</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter9_pointers-and-c-style-arrays_array-size">
        <title>Array Size and Function Parameters</title>

        <p>
            An array name does not decay in every context. For example,
            <c>sizeof(scores)</c> measures the entire array when <c>scores</c>
            is an actual array in that scope. Once the array has decayed to a
            pointer parameter, <c>sizeof(values)</c> measures the pointer, not
            the original array. This is another reason to pass the length
            explicitly.
        </p>

        <p>
            A pointer may identify one position past the last element. Such a
            pointer is useful as a stopping position, but it must never be
            dereferenced. Pointer arithmetic outside the same array object is not
            a valid way to reach unrelated storage.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter9_pointers-and-c-style-arrays_exercise_sizeof">
        <statement>
            <p>
                What does
                <c>sizeof(scores) / sizeof(scores[0])</c> calculate when
                <c>scores</c> is an actual C-style array in the current scope?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>The number of elements in the array</p></statement><feedback><p>The total array size in bytes is divided by the size of one element in bytes.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The number of bytes in the array</p></statement><feedback><p><c>sizeof(scores)</c> alone reports the total size in bytes.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The address of the first element</p></statement><feedback><p>An address is not produced by this division.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The size of a pointer</p></statement><feedback><p>This expression is evaluated while <c>scores</c> is still an actual array.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_pointers-and-c-style-arrays_exercise_decay">
        <statement><p>Why does a function receiving <c>const int* values</c> usually need a separate length parameter?</p></statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>The pointer identifies an element but does not retain the original array's length.</p></statement><feedback><p>Array decay gives the function an address, not the array-size information.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>Because pointers cannot access array elements.</p></statement><feedback><p>Pointer indexing and dereferencing can access elements.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>Because the array is automatically copied into the function.</p></statement><feedback><p>The array is not copied into a pointer parameter.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>Because pointer arithmetic always changes the array length.</p></statement><feedback><p>Pointer arithmetic does not change the array object.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_pointers-and-c-style-arrays_parsons_decay" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide">
        <statement>
            <p>Arrange the statements to traverse two elements of an array with a pointer.</p>
        </statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>int scores[]{88, 91, 95};</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>int* current = scores;</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>std::cout &lt;&lt; *current &lt;&lt; '\n';</cline></block>
            <block order="4"><cline>++current;</cline></block>
            <block order="5"><cline>std::cout &lt;&lt; *current &lt;&lt; '\n';</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_pointers-and-c-style-arrays_exercise_increment">
        <statement><p>What does <c>current + 1</c> mean when <c>current</c> is an <c>int*</c> into an array?</p></statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>A pointer to the next <c>int</c> element</p></statement><feedback><p>Pointer arithmetic is scaled by the pointed-to type.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>A pointer exactly one byte later</p></statement><feedback><p>The movement is the size of an <c>int</c>, not necessarily one byte.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The next element's integer value</p></statement><feedback><p>The result is still a pointer; dereference it to read a value.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The array's length increased by one</p></statement><feedback><p>Pointer arithmetic does not resize an array.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_pointers-and-c-style-arrays_exercise_decay_call">
        <statement><p>What does <c>scores</c> provide when passed to <c>void print_values(const int* values, std::size_t count)</c>?</p></statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>A pointer to the first element of the array</p></statement><feedback><p>In this function call, the array expression converts to a pointer to its first element.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>A copy of the entire array including its length</p></statement><feedback><p>The array is not copied into the pointer parameter, and the length is separate.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>A pointer to the last element</p></statement><feedback><p>Array decay identifies the first element.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>A <c>std::vector&lt;int&gt;</c> object</p></statement><feedback><p>A C-style array does not automatically become a vector.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_pointers-and-c-style-arrays_exercise_pointer_bounds">
        <statement><p>Why must a traversal stop before dereferencing one-past-the-end?</p></statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>One-past-the-end can be used for comparison but does not identify an element.</p></statement><feedback><p>Dereferencing is valid only for pointers to actual array elements.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>One-past-the-end always points to the first element of another array.</p></statement><feedback><p>It is a boundary position, not an element of another array.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>Pointer arithmetic stops working after the first element.</p></statement><feedback><p>Pointer arithmetic can traverse the array within its bounds.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The array length changes when the pointer reaches the boundary.</p></statement><feedback><p>Pointer movement does not change the array object.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>
</section>
